SCANF(3)                       P32W Unix                        SCANF(3)

NAME
       scanf, fscanf, sscanf, vscanf, vfscanf, vsscanf - formatted input

SYNOPSIS
       #include <stdio.h>

       int scanf(const char *fmt, ...);
       int fscanf(FILE *f, const char *fmt, ...);
       int sscanf(const char *s, const char *fmt, ...);
       int vscanf(const char *fmt, va_list ap);
       int vfscanf(FILE *f, const char *fmt, va_list ap);
       int vsscanf(const char *s, const char *fmt, va_list ap);

DESCRIPTION
       These read characters (from stdin, the stream f, or the
       string s), match them against the format fmt, and store the
       values converted through the pointer arguments, in order.

       In the format, white space matches any amount of white space
       in the input, including none. An ordinary character must
       match the next input character. A % starts a conversion:

           %[*][width][size]conversion

       * reads the field but stores nothing, and does not count it.
       width is the most characters to read for the field. size is
       hh (char), h (short) or l (long, which is the same as int
       here) for the integer conversions.

       d       a decimal integer, with an optional sign  (int *)
       i       an integer in C's notation: 0x hexadecimal, a
               leading 0 octal, else decimal  (int *)
       u       an unsigned decimal integer  (unsigned *)
       o       an octal integer  (unsigned *)
       x, X    a hexadecimal integer, 0x optional  (unsigned *)
       p       a pointer, in hexadecimal  (void **)
       c       width characters (1 by default), white space
               included; no NUL is added  (char *)
       s       a word: characters up to white space; a NUL is
               added  (char *)
       [set]   the longest run of characters in set; [^set], of
               characters not in it. ] first is a member, and a-z a
               range. A NUL is added  (char *)
       n       stores how many characters have been read so far;
               not counted  (int *)
       e, f, g, a (and E F G A)
               a floating number as strtod(3) reads it, correctly
               rounded (double *; float * too: the same type here;
               l and L are accepted)
       %       matches a %

       Every conversion but c, [ and n skips white space first.
       Give s and [ a width smaller than the array, or they can
       overrun it.

RETURN VALUE
       The number of fields stored. That is fewer than asked for
       when the input stops matching (the character that did not
       match is left unread), and EOF if the input ended before
       the first conversion.

EXAMPLES
       int h, m;
       char name[32];
       if (sscanf(line, "%d:%d", &h, &m) == 2) ...
       while (fscanf(f, "%31s %d", name, &n) == 2) ...
       sscanf(s, "name=%31[^;];uid=%d", name, &uid);

SEE ALSO
       printf(3), atoi(3), getc(3), stdio(3), stdarg(3)

BUGS
       A number longer than 512 characters is cut short.

       A stream gives back only one character (ungetc), so after
       0x not followed by a hexadecimal digit, %x and %i read the
       value 0 and the x is used up.

       Integers past 32 bits wrap around without an error.
